(*Objects* are Python?s abstraction for data. All data in a Python program is represented by) Tj T*
(objects or by relations between objects. \(In a sense, and in conformance to Von Neumann?s) Tj T*
(model of a ?stored program computer?, code is also represented by objects.\) Every object has) Tj T*
(an identity, a type and a value. An object?s *identity* never changes once it has been) Tj T*
(created; you may think of it as the object?s address in memory. The "is" operator compares) Tj T*
(the identity of two objects; the "id\(\)" function returns an integer representing its) Tj T*
(identity. An object?s type determines the operations that the object supports \(e.g., ?does) Tj T*
(it have a length??\) and also defines the possible values for objects of that type. The) Tj T*
("type\(\)" function returns an object?s type \(which is an object itself\). Like its identity,) Tj T*
(an object?s *type* is also unchangeable. [1] The *value* of some objects can change. Objects) Tj T*
(whose value can change are said to be *mutable*; objects whose value is unchangeable once) Tj T*
(they are created are called *immutable*. \(The value of an immutable container object that) Tj T*
(contains a reference to a mutable object can change when the latter?s value is changed;) Tj T*
(however the container is still considered immutable, because the collection of objects it) Tj T*
(contains cannot be changed. So, immutability is not strictly the same as having an) Tj T*
(unchangeable value, it is more subtle.\) An object?s mutability is determined by its type;) Tj T*
(for instance, numbers, strings and tuples are immutable, while dictionaries and lists are) Tj T*
(mutable. Objects are never explicitly destroyed; however, when they become unreachable they) Tj T*
(may be garbage-collected. An implementation is allowed to postpone garbage collection or) Tj T*
(omit it altogether ? it is a matter of implementation quality how garbage collection is) Tj T*
(implemented, as long as no objects are collected that are still reachable. **CPython) Tj T*
(implementation detail:** CPython currently uses a reference- counting scheme with \(optional\)) Tj T*
(delayed detection of cyclically linked garbage, which collects most objects as soon as they) Tj T*
(become unreachable, but is not guaranteed to collect garbage containing circular references.) Tj T*
(See the documentation of the "gc" module for information on controlling the collection of) Tj T*
(cyclic garbage. Other implementations act differently and CPython may change. Do not depend) Tj T*
(on immediate finalization of objects when they become unreachable \(so you should always) Tj T*
(close files explicitly\).) Tj T*
