(precise, this executes the setup statement once, and then returns the time it takes to) Tj T*
(execute the main statement a number of times, as float seconds if using the default timer.) Tj T*
(The argument is the number of times through the loop, defaulting to one million. The main) Tj T*
(statement, the setup statement and the timer function to be used are passed to the) Tj T*
(constructor. This is a convenience function that calls the timeit\(\) repeatedly, returning a) Tj T*
(list of results. The first argument specifies how many times to call timeit\(\), defaulting to) Tj T*
(5; the second argument specifies the timer argument, defaulting to one million. Note: it's) Tj T*
(tempting to calculate mean and standard deviation from the result vector and report these.) Tj T*
(However, this is not very useful. In a typical case, the lowest value gives a lower bound) Tj T*
(for how fast your machine can run the given code snippet; higher values in the result vector) Tj T*
(are typically not caused by variability in Python's speed, but by other processes) Tj T*
(interfering with your timing accuracy. So the min\(\) of the result is probably the only) Tj T*
(number you should be interested in. After that, you should look at the entire vector and) Tj T*
(apply common sense rather than statistics.) Tj T*
