(The target object thus produced, if any, is called the new target object. It is compared) Tj T*
(with the old target object \(or None, if there was no old target widget\). There are several) Tj T*
(cases \('source' is the source object, and 'event' is the most recent event object\): Once) Tj T*
(this is done, the new target object replaces the old one, and the Tk mainloop proceeds. The) Tj T*
(return value of the methods mentioned above is ignored; if they raise an exception, the) Tj T*
(normal exception handling mechanisms take over. The drag-and-drop processes can end in two) Tj T*
(ways: a final target object is selected, or no final target object is selected. When a final) Tj T*
(target object is selected, it will always have been notified of the potential drop by a call) Tj T*
(to its dnd_enter\(\) method, as described above, and possibly one or more calls to its) Tj T*
(dnd_motion\(\) method; its dnd_leave\(\) method has not been called since the last call to) Tj T*
(dnd_enter\(\). The target is notified of the drop by a call to its method dnd_commit\(source,) Tj T*
(event\). Finally, the source object is notified that the drag-and-drop process is over, by a) Tj T*
(call to source.dnd_end\(target, event\), specifying either the selected target object, or None) Tj T*
(if no target object was selected. The source object can use this to implement the commit) Tj T*
(action; this is sometimes simpler than to do it in the target's dnd_commit\(\). The target's) Tj T*
(dnd_commit\(\) method could then simply be aliased to dnd_leave\(\). At any time during a dnd) Tj T*
(sequence, the application can cancel the sequence by calling the cancel\(\) method on the) Tj T*
(object returned by dnd_start\(\). This will call dnd_leave\(\) if a target is currently active;) Tj T*
(it will never call dnd_commit\(\). This module also presents tk common file dialogues, it) Tj T*
(provides interfaces to the native file dialogues available in Tk 4.2 and newer, and the) Tj T*
(directory dialogue available in Tk 8.3 and newer. These interfaces were written by Fredrik) Tj T*
(Lundh, May 1997. The 'key' argument specifies a key in the global dictionary 'dialogstates',) Tj T*
(which keeps track of the values for the directory and pattern arguments, overriding the) Tj T*
(values passed in \(it does not keep track of the default argument!\). If no key is specified,) Tj T*
(the dialog keeps no memory of previous state. Note that memory is kept even when the dialog) Tj T*
(is canceled. \(All this emulates the behavior of the Macintosh file selection dialogs.\)) Tj T*
