(Contract Compendium - Section 10) Tj T*
(Historically pip has not had any dependencies except for ``setuptools`` itself, choosing) Tj T*
(instead to implement any functionality it needed to prevent needing a dependency. However,) Tj T*
(starting with pip 1.5, we began to replace code that was implemented inside of pip with) Tj T*
(reusable libraries from PyPI. This brought the typical benefits of reusing libraries instead) Tj T*
(of reinventing the wheel like higher quality and more battle tested code, centralization of) Tj T*
(bug fixes \(particularly security sensitive ones\), and better/more features for less work.) Tj T*
(**Fragility** When pip depends on another library to function then if for whatever reason) Tj T*
(that library either isn't installed or an incompatible version is installed then pip ceases) Tj T*
(to function. This is of course true for all Python applications, however for every) Tj T*
(application *except* for pip the way you fix it is by re-running pip. Obviously, when pip) Tj T*
(can't run, you can't use pip to fix pip, so you're left having to manually resolve) Tj T*
(dependencies and installing them by hand. **Making other libraries uninstallable** One of) Tj T*
(pip's current dependencies is the ``requests`` library, for which pip requires a fairly) Tj T*
(recent version to run. If pip depended on ``requests`` in the traditional manner, then we'd) Tj T*
(either have to maintain compatibility with every ``requests`` version that has ever existed) Tj T*
(\(and ever will\), OR allow pip to render certain versions of ``requests`` uninstallable. \(The) Tj T*
(second issue, although technically true for any Python application, is magnified by pip's) Tj T*
(ubiquity; pip is installed by default in Python, in ``pyvenv``, and in ``virtualenv``.\)) Tj T*
(**Security** This might seem puzzling at first glance, since vendoring has a tendency to) Tj T*
(complicate updating dependencies for security updates, and that holds true for pip. However,) Tj T*
(given the *other* reasons for avoiding dependencies, the alternative is for pip to reinvent) Tj T*
(the wheel itself. This is what pip did historically. It forced pip to re-implement its own) Tj T*
(HTTPS verification routines as a workaround for the Python standard library's lack of SSL) Tj T*
(validation, which resulted in similar bugs in the validation routine in ``requests`` and) Tj T*
(``urllib3``, except that they had to be discovered and fixed independently. Even though) Tj T*
(we're vendoring, reusing libraries keeps pip more secure by relying on the great work of our) Tj T*
(dependencies, *and* allowing for faster, easier security fixes by simply pulling in newer) Tj T*
(versions of dependencies.) Tj T*
(Page 378) Tj T*
