(Vendoring is automated via the `vendoring <https://pypi.org/project/vendoring/>`_ tool from) Tj T*
(the content of ``pip/_vendor/vendor.txt`` and the different patches in) Tj T*
(``tools/vendoring/patches``. Launch it via ``vendoring sync . -v`` \(requires) Tj T*
(``vendoring>=0.2.2``\). Tool configuration is done via ``pyproject.toml``. The ``vendoring``) Tj T*
(tool automatically applies our local patches, but updating, the patches sometimes no longer) Tj T*
(apply cleanly. In that case, the update will fail. To resolve this, take the following) Tj T*
(steps: 1. Revert any incomplete changes in the revendoring branch, to ensure you have a) Tj T*
(clean starting point. 2. Run the revendoring of the library with a problem again: ``nox -s) Tj T*
(vendoring -- --upgrade <library_name>``. 3. This will fail again, but you will have the) Tj T*
(original source in your working directory. Review the existing patch against the source, and) Tj T*
(modify the patch to reflect the new version of the source. If you ``git add`` the changes) Tj T*
(the vendoring made, you can modify the source to reflect the patch file and then generate a) Tj T*
(new patch with ``git diff``. 4. Now, revert everything *except* the patch file changes.) Tj T*
(Leave the modified patch file unstaged but saved in the working tree. 5. Re-run the) Tj T*
(vendoring. This time, it should pick up the changed patch file and apply it cleanly. The) Tj T*
(patch file changes will be committed along with the revendoring, so the new commit should be) Tj T*
(ready to test and publish as a PR. As mentioned in the rationale, we, the pip team, would) Tj T*
(prefer it if pip was not debundled \(other than optionally) Tj T*
(``pip/_vendor/requests/cacert.pem``\) and that pip was left intact. However, if you insist on) Tj T*
(doing so, we have a semi-supported method \(that we don't test in our CI\) and requires a bit) Tj T*
(of extra work on your end in order to solve the problems described above. 1. Delete) Tj T*
(everything in ``pip/_vendor/`` **except** for ``pip/_vendor/__init__.py`` and) Tj T*
(``pip/_vendor/vendor.txt``. 2. Generate wheels for each of pip's dependencies \(and any of) Tj T*
(their dependencies\) using your patched copies of these libraries. These must be placed) Tj T*
(somewhere on the filesystem that pip can access \(``pip/_vendor`` is the default assumption\).) Tj T*
(3. Modify ``pip/_vendor/__init__.py`` so that the ``DEBUNDLED`` variable is ``True``. 4.) Tj T*
(Upon installation, the ``INSTALLER`` file in pip's own ``dist-info`` directory should be set) Tj T*
(to something other than ``pip``, so that pip can detect that it wasn't installed using) Tj T*
(itself. 5. *\(optional\)* If you've placed the wheels in a location other than) Tj T*
(``pip/_vendor/``, then modify ``pip/_vendor/__init__.py`` so that the ``WHEEL_DIR`` variable) Tj T*
(points to the location you've placed them. 6. *\(optional\)* Update the) Tj T*
(``pip_self_version_check`` logic to use the appropriate logic for determining the latest) Tj T*
(available version of pip and prompt the user with the correct upgrade message.) Tj T*
